<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>7.11.4: Using the Vertex and Axis of Symmetry of Quadratics</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01930</md:content-id>
    <md:title>7.11.4: Using the Vertex and Axis of Symmetry of Quadratics</md:title>
    <md:uuid>5201447f-8e34-47de-b0a0-55c039441731</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section id="sect-00001">
      <title>Activity</title>
      <para id="para-00001">While answering the questions below, plot each point on a coordinate plane with grid paper by hand. Do not use technology.</para>
      <para id="para-00002">For questions 1 – 4, use the the function <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>4</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>.</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math></para>
      <!--Q#1-->
      <exercise id="exer-00001">
        <problem id="prob-00001">
          <para id="para-00003"> Find the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-coordinates of the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-intercepts of <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00001">
          <para id="para-00005">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00006"><m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>4</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> and <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Q#2-->
      <exercise id="exer-00002">
        <problem id="prob-00002">
          <para id="para-00007"> Find the coordinate of the vertex.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00002">
          <para id="para-00009">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00004"><m:math><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>5</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math></para></solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Q#3-->
      
        <exercise id="exer-00003">
          <problem id="prob-00003">
            <para id="para-00011"> On your graph, sketch a quadratic through the key features on your graph and draw a vertical line through the vertex using a different color.</para>
            <para id="para-00012">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00003">
            <para id="para-00013">Compare your answer:</para>
            <para id="para-00014">
              
                <media alt="Insert Alt Text">
                  <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_08_13_09_01.jpg"/>
                </media>
              
          </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Q#4-->
      <exercise id="exer-00004">
        <problem id="prob-00004">
          <para id="para-00015"> What is the equation of the vertical line that goes through the vertex?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00004">
          <para id="para-00017">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00008"><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>1</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para></solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Q#5-->
      <exercise id="exer-00005">
        <problem id="prob-00005">
          <para id="para-00019"> What does the vertical line that goes through the vertex do to the parabola?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00005">
          <para id="para-00021">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00022">Your answer may vary, but here is a sample. The vertical line <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>1</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> cuts the parabola in half and creates two symmetrical parts.</para>
          <para id="para-00023">This imaginary vertical line that goes through the vertex and cuts the parabola in half is called the <term class="glossary-tooltip" id="term-00001">axis of symmetry</term>.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Q#6-->
      
        <exercise id="exer-00006">
          <problem id="prob-00006">
            <para id="para-00024"> On your graph, label your <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-intercepts, vertex, and axis of symmetry. Identify your vertex as a maximum or minimum.</para>
            <para id="para-00025">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00006">
            <para id="para-00026">Compare your answer:</para>
            <para id="para-00027">
              
                <media alt="Insert Alt Text">
                  <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_08_13_09_02.jpg"/>
                </media>
              
          </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Q#7-->
      <exercise id="exer-00007">
        <problem id="prob-00007">
          <para id="para-00028"> Given a vertex of <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>4</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math> and a point <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math>, what is another point on the quadratic?</para>
          <para id="para-00029">(Hint: It is helpful to draw a picture and use the axis of symmetry).</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00007">
          <para id="para-00031">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00032">Your answer may vary, but here is a sample.</para>
          <para id="para-00033">
            
              <media alt="Insert Alt Text">
                <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_08_13_09_03.jpg"/>
              </media>
            
        </para>
          <para id="para-00034">Since <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mn>4</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math> is the vertex, then <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> is the axis of symmetry. If <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math> is 3 units away from the axis of symmetry, there is another point 3 units to the left of the axis of symmetry. This is the point <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>5</m:mn><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
    </section>

    <note class="self-check" id="note-00001">
      <para id="para-00035"> What is the equation of the axis of symmetry for <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math>?</para>
    </note>

    <section id="sect-00002">
      <title>Axis of Symmetry of a Quadratic </title>
      <para id="para-00036">In the graph of a quadratic function, the axis of symmetry is an imaginary line that cuts the parabola in half to create two symmetrical sides. It goes through the vertex of the parabola so the equation of the axis of symmetry is the same as the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-coordinate of the vertex.</para>
      <para id="para-00037">
          <media alt="Insert Alt Text">
            <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_08_13_11_01.jpg"/>
          </media>
        
        <emphasis id="docs-internal-guid-1a59f64e-7fff-f686-b37e-3c95d10108a1"/>
    </para>
      <para id="para-00038">Notice that in Figure 1, the vertex is at <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math> and the axis of symmetry is at <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
      <para id="para-00039">In Figure 2, the vertex is at <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mn>7</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math> and the axis of symmetry is at <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
      <para id="para-00040">
       <emphasis>Example</emphasis>
    </para>
      <para id="para-00041">Find the vertex and axis of symmetry for <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>6</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
      <para id="para-00042"><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> -  Find the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-intercepts.
  Set each factor equal to 0.
  <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>
  <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>
  <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>6</m:mn><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>
  <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>6</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para>
      <para id="para-00043"><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Find the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-coordinate of the vertex.
  The vertex is halfway between the two <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-intercepts.
  <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>6</m:mn><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:msup><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>
  <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mfrac><m:mrow><m:mn>6</m:mn><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow><m:mrow><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:mrow></m:mfrac><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para>
      <para id="para-00044"><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Find the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-coordinate of the vertex.
  Substitute the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-coordinate into <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math>.
  <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>6</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>4</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>4</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>6</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>
  The vertex is at <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>16</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
      <para id="para-00045"><emphasis>Step 4</emphasis> - Find the axis of symmetry.
  The axis of symmetry is the equation where <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> equals the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-coordinate of the function.
  <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para>
      <note class="try" id="note-00002">
  <title>Try It: Axis of Symmetry of a Quadratic</title>
      <para id="para-00046">For questions 1 - 2, use the equation of the quadratic, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>4</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>10</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
      <!--Q#1-->
      <exercise id="exer-00008">
        <problem id="prob-00008">
          <para id="para-00047">What is the vertex of the quadratic?</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00008">
          <para id="para-00048">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00010"><m:math><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>7</m:mn><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>9</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math></para><para id="para-00050"><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - Find the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-intercepts.
      Set each factor equal to 0.
      <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>4</m:mn><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>
      <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>4</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>
      <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>10</m:mn><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>
      <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>10</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para>
          <para id="para-00051"><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Find the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-coordinate of the vertex.
      The vertex is halfway between the two <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-intercepts.
      <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mfrac><m:mrow><m:mn>4</m:mn><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>10</m:mn></m:mrow><m:mrow><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:mrow></m:mfrac><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>7</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para>
          <para id="para-00052"><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Find the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-coordinate of the vertex.
      Substitute the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-coordinate into <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math>.
      <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>7</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>7</m:mn><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>4</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>7</m:mn><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>10</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>9</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>
      The vertex is at <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>7</m:mn><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>9</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Q#2-->
      <exercise id="exer-00009">
        <problem id="prob-00009">
          <para id="para-00053">What is the axis of symmetry of the quadratic?</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00009">
          <para id="para-00054">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00016"><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>7</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para><para id="para-00056">The axis of symmetry is the equation where <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> equals the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-coordinate of the vertex.
      <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>7</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para>
        </solution>
      </exercise></note>
      <!--Interaction End -->
    </section>
  </content></document>